{"schema_version":3,"catalogue_version":1,"id":"wcf:21.8","provision":"WCF 21.8","book":"wcf","book_name":"Westminster Confession of Faith","abbreviation":"WCF","reference":"21.8","title":"Section 21.8","supplementary":false,"canonical_url":"https://raymond-rishty.github.io/pca-ga/provisions/wcf/21.8/","reader_url":"https://raymond-rishty.github.io/pca-constitution-reader/#wcf/21.8","current_text":{"html":"<p>This Sabbath is then kept holy unto the Lord, when men, after a due preparing of their hearts, and ordering of their common affairs beforehand, do not only observe an holy rest, all the day, from their own works, words, and thoughts about their worldly employments and recreations, but also are taken up, the whole time, in the public and private exercises of his worship, and in the duties of necessity and mercy.</p>","text":"This Sabbath is then kept holy unto the Lord, when men, after a due preparing of their hearts, and ordering of their common affairs beforehand, do not only observe an holy rest, all the day, from their own works, words, and thoughts about their worldly employments and recreations, but also are taken up, the whole time, in the public and private exercises of his worship, and in the duties of necessity and mercy."},"source":{"name":"PCA Constitution Reader","revision":"14d73f7e5caf1d22e0514870dd30fcc69f8165cc","edition":"Current Reader text","files":{"bco.js":"c88e552545b73fd9690de620b237895b7ea52a94bed8e95690c116bf76be8ae5","wcf.js":"1ebf9e54223f7fe4e728a3e640c833a435af825d74b5ef3d4b04687685538a0d","wlc.js":"f6977e761ea4ccc93b07fcacfd0391890cb05b42f4317d807fa293c078d9b051","wsc.js":"dd775417f7ef21e3283bacad5e6ef193c41d64d03bd5a2e8fb934ccca8cb8e53","rao.js":"49808bbb0e95abdc3b07506c87dde0d62d087851e869b49c0f1f2424986450ec"}},"input_fingerprint":"38e35af46c8cbdb12a9a00c82d72f7192575b5ec27348534148f2a834551edc4","assessment_fingerprint":"517b100d2e9006e313ab6d84d4f0f31593b77832f395383ba2227779470cd034","assessment_summary":{"status":"stale","file":"index/provision_link_adjudications.jsonl","total":11481,"expected":12454,"applied":0,"stale":11481,"unmatched":0,"unassessed":12454},"parent_id":"wcf:21","children":[],"coverage":{"relationships":"indexed_records","recommendations":{"status":"incomplete","note":"Recommendation and study papers are not comprehensively indexed by provision."},"history":"not_available"},"relationship_count":16,"relationships":[{"id":"wcf:21.8--case:cases/ga51_2024__2023-04.md","record_id":"case:cases/ga51_2024__2023-04.md","type":"Judicial case","title":"Biese et al. v. Tennessee Valley Presbytery","year":2024,"disposition":"denied","authority_weight":"high","url":"https://raymond-rishty.github.io/pca-ga/cases/ga51_2024__2023-04.html","raw_url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/raymond-rishty/pca-ga/main/cases/ga51_2024__2023-04.md","evidence_basis":"direct_text","relevance_status":"unreviewed","occurrences":[{"id":"wcf:21.8--case:cases/ga51_2024__2023-04.md:occ-4923c87980ce","url":"https://raymond-rishty.github.io/pca-ga/cases/ga51_2024__2023-04.html","repository_path":"cases/ga51_2024__2023-04.md","locator":{"fragment":null,"line":38},"excerpt":"…minutes be cited for an exception of substance based on WLC 117, WLC 118, WCF 21:8, and BCO 40-2. After floor debate, the RSR Committee motion failed. A subsequent motion to approve the Redeemer Church minutes without exception carried.","sources":["case_markdown_text"],"raw_url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/raymond-rishty/pca-ga/main/cases/ga51_2024__2023-04.md"}],"reference_presentation":{"group":"review","label":null},"metadata":{"topics":["Sabbath observance","Super Bowl party","review and control","session records review","SJC jurisdiction","exception of substance","Westminster Confession 21:8","WLC 117","WLC 118","BCO 40-2","BCO 40-3","non-justiciability","adjacent courts","RAO Chapter 16"],"standard_of_review":"not_stated","case_numbers":["2023-4"],"body":"SJC","synopsis":"Biese and others complained that Tennessee Valley Presbytery erred by approving Redeemer Church session minutes without exception—minutes noting a youth Super Bowl party—and by denying their complaint seeking a Sabbath exception. The SJC denied the complaint: because Presbytery gave no reasons for its actions, the record was insufficient to find it erred in interpreting the Constitution. A concurrence said the denial did not decide the Sabbath merits; a dissent would have held the complaint judicially out of order for lack of SJC jurisdiction over presbytery record review."}},{"id":"wcf:21.8--case:cases/ga48_2021__2019-02.md","record_id":"case:cases/ga48_2021__2019-02.md","type":"Judicial case","title":"Schrock et al. v. Philadelphia Presbytery","year":2021,"disposition":"sustained","authority_weight":"high","url":"https://raymond-rishty.github.io/pca-ga/cases/ga48_2021__2019-02.html","raw_url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/raymond-rishty/pca-ga/main/cases/ga48_2021__2019-02.md","evidence_basis":"direct_text","relevance_status":"unreviewed","occurrences":[{"id":"wcf:21.8--case:cases/ga48_2021__2019-02.md:occ-5b9b5779a5e2","url":"https://raymond-rishty.github.io/pca-ga/cases/ga48_2021__2019-02.html","repository_path":"cases/ga48_2021__2019-02.md","locator":{"fragment":null,"line":86},"excerpt":"…Standards on the doctrines of creation (WCF 4.1) and the Fourth Commandment (WCF 21.8). While those statements are brief, they are in TE's Smith's own words, and the Presbytery took a specific action required by BCO 21-4 and RAO 16, namely, to judge those…","sources":["case_markdown_text"],"raw_url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/raymond-rishty/pca-ga/main/cases/ga48_2021__2019-02.md"}],"reference_presentation":{"group":"review","label":null},"metadata":{"topics":["ministerial exam","transfer from another denomination","stated differences","WCF 1.1","continuationism","cessationism","prophecy","tongues","spiritual gifts","RAO 16-3.e.5","BCO 21-4","fundamentals of the system of doctrine"],"standard_of_review":"not_stated","case_numbers":["2019-2"],"body":"SJC","synopsis":"The SJC sustained a complaint against Philadelphia Presbytery for failing to judge and record TE Larry Smith’s stated difference over the continuation of prophecy and tongues beyond the apostolic era. The record showed Presbytery approved Smith’s transfer exam and defeated a motion to find his view out of accord, but it did not categorize that view in Smith’s own words. The SJC remanded for a written statement and Presbytery’s categorical judgment, without undoing Smith’s transfer or deciding whether his view was erroneous."}},{"id":"wcf:21.8--case:cases/ga29_2001__1999-07.md","record_id":"case:cases/ga29_2001__1999-07.md","type":"Judicial case","title":"Jeffrey M. Black v. Eastern Carolina Presbytery","year":2001,"disposition":"not_sustained","authority_weight":"high","url":"https://raymond-rishty.github.io/pca-ga/cases/ga29_2001__1999-07.html","raw_url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/raymond-rishty/pca-ga/main/cases/ga29_2001__1999-07.md","evidence_basis":"direct_text","relevance_status":"unreviewed","occurrences":[{"id":"wcf:21.8--case:cases/ga29_2001__1999-07.md:occ-3e6ccd213e84","url":"https://raymond-rishty.github.io/pca-ga/cases/ga29_2001__1999-07.html","repository_path":"cases/ga29_2001__1999-07.md","locator":{"fragment":null,"line":54},"excerpt":"…cases involving constitutional interpretation and the issues of the Sabbath (WCF 21-8) and theonomy (WCF 19-4).","sources":["case_markdown_text"],"raw_url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/raymond-rishty/pca-ga/main/cases/ga29_2001__1999-07.md"},{"id":"wcf:21.8--case:cases/ga29_2001__1999-07.md:occ-825d9fe73626","url":"https://raymond-rishty.github.io/pca-ga/cases/ga29_2001__1999-07.html","repository_path":"cases/ga29_2001__1999-07.md","locator":{"fragment":null,"line":56},"excerpt":"…WCF 21-8. Presbytery denied the complaint and judged there was no violation of WCF 21-8 and \"no impropriety in the use of the Lord's Day.\" A GA Judicial Commission affirmed the Presbytery's judgment and denied the complaint. The Assembly approved the…","sources":["case_markdown_text"],"raw_url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/raymond-rishty/pca-ga/main/cases/ga29_2001__1999-07.md"},{"id":"wcf:21.8--case:cases/ga29_2001__1999-07.md:occ-9fdcbed21783","url":"https://raymond-rishty.github.io/pca-ga/cases/ga29_2001__1999-07.html","repository_path":"cases/ga29_2001__1999-07.md","locator":{"fragment":null,"line":56},"excerpt":"…children on the church playground. The complaint alleged the Session violated WCF 21-8. Presbytery denied the complaint and judged there was no violation of WCF 21-8 and \"no impropriety in the use of the Lord's Day.\" A GA Judicial Commission affirmed the…","sources":["case_markdown_text"],"raw_url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/raymond-rishty/pca-ga/main/cases/ga29_2001__1999-07.md"}],"reference_presentation":{"group":"review","label":null},"metadata":{"topics":["creation days","Westminster Standards exception","transfer examination","exceptions to Westminster Standards"],"standard_of_review":"mixed","review_standards":["factual_findings","discretion_and_judgment"],"case_numbers":["1999-7"],"body":"SJC","synopsis":"Black challenged Presbytery’s handling of his complaint and its ruling that a licentiate’s non-24-hour creation-day view was not an exception to the Westminster Standards. The SJC denied remand because Presbytery had effectively adjudicated the complaint; the separate objection to proceeding without Black was not first filed below and was administratively out of order. It upheld Presbytery’s ruling that not every non-Calendar-Day view constitutes an exception. Because the record did not identify the licentiate’s precise view, the decision did not determine its acceptability or whether it could be taught."}},{"id":"wcf:21.8--overture:ga43_2015:2","record_id":"overture:ga43_2015:2","type":"Overture","title":"Form Ad Interim Committee to Revise Westminster Standards on Sabbath","year":2015,"disposi